  <title>Zebra label designer</title>

  <p><guiseq><gui>Edit</gui><gui>Zebra label designer</gui></guiseq> draws the
  label templates that product and serial number labels print with on a Zebra
  label printer. It asks for the administrator password first. The
  <gui>Edit</gui> button beside a template in the label windows opens the
  same designer on that template.</p>

  <p>The designer is the LinuxZPL program, built into PyGtk Posting. Its own
  menus draw, align and preview the label. PyGtk Posting adds the
  <gui>Database</gui> menu, which keeps the templates every computer prints
  from:</p>

  <terms>
    <item><title><gui>Open template…</gui>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>O</key></keyseq>)</title>
    <p>Opens a stored template. When templates exist, the designer offers
    this as soon as it opens.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Save template</gui></title>
    <p>Saves the open template.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Save template as…</gui></title>
    <p>Saves it as a new template; give it a name and say whether it is a
    product or a serial number label. Only that kind of label window lists
    it.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Rename template…</gui>, <gui>Delete template…</gui></title>
    <p>Rename or delete the open template, for every computer.</p></item>
  </terms>

  <p>The window title shows the template's name, with a <gui>*</gui> when
  there are unsaved changes; beneath it, whether the label lives in the
  database or in a file. <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>S</key></keyseq> saves to
  wherever it lives. The <gui>File</gui> menu works on .zpl files on this
  computer instead.</p>

  <note><p>The designer asks the label printer set in <link
  xref="settings#zebra">the settings</link> for its resolution. If it cannot
  reach the printer it falls back to the last resolution it found; a template
  designed at the wrong resolution prints the wrong size.</p></note>

